    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Yordan Alvarez**, who enters the day with a high-ceiling score of 77.04. Facing Kendry Rojas and his shaky 5.83 xERA, **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a massive +19% SP Skill boost that outweighs the negative impact of a same-handed platoon, making him our primary engine for counting stats. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** also provides significant upside (73.09) against Brandon Sproat. While he faces an \"Elite Hold\" pitcher\u2014which traditionally penalizes SB projections\u2014his ability to leverage a favorable ballpark and dynamic platoon advantage makes him a high-efficiency start, despite the late-order spot in the lineup.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Luke Raley** (48.26). He narrowly missed the cut behind the high-scoring **Junior Caminero** (59.25), largely because the optimizer favored the high-volume lineup spots for the rest of the infield over **Luke Raley**\u2019s difficult matchup against Noah Schultz. Similarly, **Hyeseong Kim** (47.68) remains on the bench despite a strong profile, as his projected #9 spot and Michael King\u2019s matchup difficulty suppress his efficiency score below our threshold for active play.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding external variables today. **Dillon Dingler**, **Colt Keith**, and **Yordan Alvarez** are all flagged for rain risk, with the former two facing a potential washout against Slade Cecconi. If those games are delayed or postponed, we will need to re-evaluate the bench. Additionally, **Trea Turner** (70.06) and **Kyle Schwarber** (68.29) are our primary weapons against the tough arm of Nick Lodolo; their ability to capitalize on wind-out conditions and favorable order slots makes them must-starts despite the caliber of the opposing pitcher. **Zac Veen** remains sidelined in the minors, so ensure that no roster spots are left empty if late-breaking lineup news forces a swap for either **Chase Meidroth** or our other available depth pieces.",
